Java 8 introduced Optional class in the java.util package to provide a better way to handle situations where a value might be absent
helping to avoid NullPointerException and making the code more expressive
Optional is a container object that may or may not contain a non-null value. It's a way to explicitly express that a method
might not return a value, forcing the caller to handle both cases.

A. Key benefits of Optional
1. Explicitly indicates that a value might be absent
2. Reduces null check in code
3. Provides a fluent API for handling absent values
4. Makes the intent of your code clearer

B. Creating Optional Objects
1. Empty Optional
Optional<String> emptyOptional=Optional.empty();

2. Optional with non-null value
Optional<String> nonEmptyOptional=Optional.of("something");
//throws NullPointerException if value is null

3. Optional that may be null
String possibleNull=null;
Optional<String> nullableOptional=Optional.ofNullable(possibleNull);
//Accepts null values without throwing exception

C. Checking Value Presence

1. isPresent()

Optional<String> opt = Optional.of("Java");
if (opt.isPresent()) {
    System.out.println("Value: " + opt.get());
}

2. isEmpty()

Optional<String> opt = Optional.empty();
if (opt.isEmpty()) {
    System.out.println("No value present");
}

D. Retrieving Values

1. get() Not Recommended (can throw NoSuchElementException)

Optional<String> opt = Optional.of("Hello");
String value = opt.get(); // "Hello"

2. orElse() Provide default value
The fallback value is computed or evaluated every time, even if the Optional is not empty

Optional<String> opt = Optional.empty();
String value = opt.orElse("Default"); // "Default", if value present then gives that value

Optional<String> name = Optional.ofNullable(null);  // Empty Optional
String result = name.orElse("Default Name");
System.out.println(result);  // Output: Default Name

Optional<String> anotherName = Optional.of("John Doe");  // Non-empty Optional
String anotherResult = anotherName.orElse("Default Name");
System.out.println(anotherResult);  // Output: John Doe

3. orElseGet() Lazy default with Supplier
it evaluates the fallback value lazily-only when the Optional is empty, while orElse always evaluates the fallback value
even when it is not needed.

Optional<String> opt = Optional.empty();
String value = opt.orElseGet(() -> expensiveOperation()); // Only called if needed
Optional<String> name = Optional.ofNullable(null);

// Using orElse (returns default directly)
String resultWithOrElse = name.orElse("Default Name");
System.out.println(resultWithOrElse);  // Output: Default Name
// Using orElseGet (computes default lazily)
String resultWithOrElseGet = name.orElseGet(() -> "Lazy Default Name");
System.out.println(resultWithOrElseGet);  // Output: Lazy Default Name (computed lazily)

4. orElseThrow Throw exception if empty
You want to ensure that a value is present in the Optional and explicitly handle the case where it is missing by throwing an exception
It provides a clear way to fail fast in situations where the absence of a value is considered as error and must be handled immediately
You need to throw custom exceptions for more meaningful error messages or specific application
String result = value.orElseThrow(() -> new CustomException("Value not found"));

Optional<String> opt = Optional.empty();
String value = opt.orElseThrow(() -> new RuntimeException("Value not present"));

E. Conditional Actions

1. ifPresent() Execute action if value exists
Typically used when you want to perform an action only when a value is present (sending notification, making a database update)

Optional<String> opt = Optional.of("Hello");
opt.ifPresent(val -> System.out.println("Value: " + val));// prints to console

Optional<String> emptyOptional=Optional.empty();
emptyOptional.ifPresent(value->System.out.println("this is the value "+value));//does nothing as value is absent

2. ifPresentOrElse Execute action for both cases
Used when you need to perform one action if the value is present and a different action when it is absent
eg if user has credit card then make payment, else notify user to add credit card

Optional<String> opt = Optional.empty();
opt.ifPresentOrElse(
    val -> System.out.println("Value: " + val),
    () -> System.out.println("No value")
);